<spell-notebook "text usage">
	<header level="1">
		Text
		<header>A text is used to style some inline text with a simple color.</>
	</>
	<text-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-text>` is an inline span that flows with the text around it: wrap the words, then add `color`, `state`,
			`size`, `inverted` or `disabled`.

			- `color` takes a palette hue (`red` ... `black`) or `primary` / `secondary`; text without one inherits its colour.
			- `state` (`info`, `success`, `warning`, `error`) is the colour of a status message.
			- `size` is relative to the surrounding text: `mini` is 0.4em, `medium` 1em, `massive` 8em.
			- It ignores the colour and size of a component it sits in: red text inside a large blue button is red, at the
			  button's text size, never scaled twice.
			- `paragraph` makes it a paragraph (`<p>`) instead:  a block, with the body's line height.
			- `link` makes it a link;  `<ui-link>` is the same as a tag of its own.  `disabled` drops the link.
			- Text has no events.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<p>Your plan renews on <ui-text color="blue">1 March</ui-text>; payment <ui-text state="error">failed</ui-text>.</p>
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>Nothing to do: a text is not focusable and takes no keys.</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- Colour is never the only signal: `state="error"` is only a colour, so the words must say it is an error too.
			  Use `role="alert"` or a live region when it has to be announced.
			- Coloured text uses each hue's text shade, tuned to reach 4.5:1 contrast on the page background in both light
			  and dark schemes. `inverted` switches to the shade meant for dark surfaces.
			- `disabled` only fades the text; it does not hide it from screen readers.
			- If the element fails to render, it shows its text through a bare `<slot>`,
			  without the class words' look.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
